(ns kotoba.io.byte-buffer
  "byte-buffer -- addressed on its own.

  Split out of kotoba.lang.io on 2026-09-09 (ADR-2609091200). The unit
  here is the DEFINITION, and this repo's deps.edn names exactly the
  definitions it reaches -- nothing else.

  KOTOBA reading (the :kotoba branch): the buffer is a `:bytes` VALUE, not a
  cell. An atom cannot cross a function boundary on the Kotoba route (atom
  slice 1: a let-bound cell, used inside its own function), and a buffer is
  exactly what put / len / to-bytes take across one. `put` returns the grown
  buffer, which the host `put` also returns, so a caller that uses put's
  RETURN value behaves the same on both; a caller that relied on the host's
  in-place mutation of a buffer it kept a reference to is the one difference.
  The :default branch is the host code, unchanged."
  )

#?(:kotoba
   (defn byte-buffer [] :bytes
     (bytes-empty))
   :default
   (defn byte-buffer
     "A pure growable byte buffer backed by a persistent vector of unsigned byte
  values (ints 0–255). Portable to WASM (no ByteArrayOutputStream)."
     []
     (atom [])))
